cs5(){return A.fl(!0,"Tiled",A.a([A.aH(new A.bDl(),"https://github.com/flame-engine/flame/blob/main/examples/lib/stories/tiled/flame_tiled_animation_example.dart","    Loads and displays an animated Tiled map.\n  ","Flame Tiled Animation"),A.aH(new A.bDm(),"https://github.com/flame-engine/flame/blob/main/examples/lib/stories/tiled/tiled_culling_example.dart",'The camera slowly moves over a big Tiled map of 400 x 400 tiles. Press C, or use\nthe "Culled" knob, to turn culling on and off. Use the "Orientation" knob to\nchange the kind of map: orthogonal, isometric, staggered or hexagonal.\n\nWith culling on, the map is split into chunks of 16 x 16 tiles (the `chunkSize`\nof the map), and only the chunks that are in view are drawn. With culling off,\nevery tile of the map is drawn on every frame, even though only a few of them\nare on-screen.\n\nThe "Map draw time" line is how long the map needs to draw in Dart. It does not\ninclude the work of the GPU. Compare it, and the frame rate, with culling on and\noff.\n  ',"Tiled Culling"),A.aH(new A.bDn(),"https://github.com/flame-engine/flame/blob/main/examples/lib/stories/tiled/tiled_culling_visualized_example.dart",'See which parts of a Tiled map are skipped when culling is on. The big view\nshows the whole map. The small view in the bottom right corner is a second\ncamera that moves around, and it is the one that does the culling. The white box\nshows what it sees.\n\nThe map is split into chunks of 16 x 16 tiles, which are the shapes of the\ngrid. A chunk is green if the small camera draws it. The small camera skips the\nchunks that are not green, since they are out of its view. Flame compares the\nview with the smallest box around each chunk, so a chunk of an isometric map\ncan be green when the view only touches the corner of its box.\n\nUse the "Orientation" knob to change the kind of map: orthogonal, isometric,\nstaggered or hexagonal.\n\nPress C, or use the "Culled" knob, to turn culling on and off. With culling off,\nthe small camera draws every chunk, so they are all green.\n\nThe FPS at the top is for the whole game. While the big view is on, it sets the\nFPS, because it always draws the whole map. Press V to hide the big view. Then\nthe FPS is the FPS of the small camera alone.\n\nThe "draw time" line is how long the small camera needs to draw the map in Dart.\nIt does not include the work of the GPU.\n  ',"Tiled Culling (visualized)")],t.l))},
